{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S6 — Clear the parity bar for Bedrock before it becomes a first-class provider\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/llm/parity.py` defines rebar's non-inferiority bar — structured-output validity, per-criterion\nverdict agreement with zero decision-level gold flips, recall and false-accept within 2pp, and error rate — and\nit was required before the last LLM substrate swap (the LangGraph → pydantic-ai cutover). Routing gate traffic\nthrough Bedrock is a substrate change of the same kind: different API surface (Converse), different capability\nflags, and a different structured-output path. Declaring Bedrock \"first-class\" without running that bar would\nmake the tier label unearned.\n\n## Approach\n\nRun the existing `parity_report` machinery with the standing eval corpus, v1 = direct Anthropic and\nv2 = Bedrock-hosted Claude [rebar:2932-8a98-6d70-4550], same model family and decoding params, N≥3 repeats — the\nprotocol `parity.py` already documents. No new gate logic is written; this story supplies the corpus run and\nrecords the report. It runs as an env-gated live job modeled on the existing weekly live-LLM jobs, not as a\nblocking CI job, because it needs funded model calls.\n\nThe report is the deliverable and it is **decision-bearing**: if Bedrock fails any criterion, this ticket\nrecords the failure and the epic's first-class claim for Bedrock is revisited before the bot cutover\nproceeds — rather than shipping a tier label the evidence does not support.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** asserting parity from the fact that it is the same underlying model. The Converse API\ndiffers in structured-output handling and cache semantics, which is precisely what criterion (a) measures.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/parity.py` — no logic change expected; wiring only if the driver needs a provider parameter.\n- `.github/workflows/` — the env-gated live parity job.\n- This ticket's comments — the recorded `ParityReport`.\n\n## Testing\n\nLLM-surface eval tier (§10): pinned model and decoding settings, paired runs differing only in provider,\nmultiple samples against the predeclared thresholds already encoded in `parity_report`, scored deterministically\nby the existing report function.\n\n- the offline gate logic stays exercised by the existing synthetic-record unit tests (no regression).\n- the live run reports both rates, not just the pass/fail bit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a `ParityReport` comparing Bedrock to direct Anthropic on the standing corpus (N≥3 repeats) is attached as\n      a comment on this ticket, including validity, agreement, recall, false-accept, and error rate\n- [ ] [operator-attested] the recorded ParityReport states pass or fail against EACH `parity.py`\n      criterion explicitly, quoted in a comment on this ticket\n- [ ] [operator-attested] if any criterion fails, a comment on this ticket records the decision taken\n      (remediate, or drop Bedrock's first-class claim)\n- [ ] [operator-attested] the epic ticket's text reflects that decision. Tagged because a ticket\n      FIELD is not a repository file and the completion verifier reads repository files only.\n      SEQUENCING HAZARD, and the reason this is worth spelling out rather than just tagging: editing\n      epic 061c's description is a MATERIAL edit to the epic, which invalidates its plan-review\n      attestation (stale-material). The epic must close AFTER all its children, so an edit made late\n      in the sequence would force a fresh review of the epic's very large plan at the worst possible\n      moment. Do this edit BEFORE the epic's final review, or record the decision as a COMMENT on\n      the epic instead — comments are not material and carry the same information\n- [ ] the live job is env-gated and does not run in default CI (`make test` unaffected)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785582810163982001,"uuid":"77e47515-067c-4160-b2f3-dbed573d8170"}